Stocks, delays, demand and the availability of recovered feed (@mat-material-stocks: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @materials-engineering, #source "../sources/Materials-Science-behind-Sustainable-Metals-Raabe.lingua#r_6MXWN12M466MS374XY585GBHBN", #source "../../../materials/metals-and-metallurgical-production/sources/Metal-Recycling-UNEP-IRP.lingua#r_40JXFY43ST442BB8MJEAYJT9N1", #source "../sources/Plastics-Recycling-Economy-NIST.lingua#r_4FMXC9V02YNV4DFSCCV0CVR8JN", #materials-engineering "Connect-material-choices-to-the-work-that-keeps-them-useful.lingua#r_0JRG7CM5616BS9TMYG9D5Q529G") { r_0AQSQAQ51ER1JFB2MJ35VJE5XZ
**Read:** [The Materials Science behind Sustainable Metals and Alloys — Dierk Raabe](../sources/Materials-Science-behind-Sustainable-Metals-Raabe.lingua#r_6MXWN12M466MS374XY585GBHBN), the complete feed/recovery and production discussion; [Metal Recycling: Opportunities, Limits, Infrastructure — UNEP International Resource Panel](../../../materials/metals-and-metallurgical-production/sources/Metal-Recycling-UNEP-IRP.lingua#r_40JXFY43ST442BB8MJEAYJT9N1); [The U.S. Plastics Recycling Economy — Thomas, Kneifel and Butry](../sources/Plastics-Recycling-Economy-NIST.lingua#r_4FMXC9V02YNV4DFSCCV0CVR8JN), infrastructure and economic context.

Material enters products that can remain in service for years. Returns depend on past inflows, lifetime distributions, collection and processing, not just present demand. With fixed lifetime τ and recovery fraction r, a simplified return is rI(t−τ); a lifetime distribution requires a convolution over previous cohorts. Growth can therefore make current recoverable feed much smaller than current material demand even at high collection efficiency.

Composition, location and available processing capacity further restrict useful supply. A recovered stock is not a throughput, and a technically recoverable constituent is not necessarily an available qualified feed. Infrastructure itself requires construction, maintenance, energy and skilled work.

**Work:** Build a cohort balance for a growing and a steady-demand system. Compare fixed and distributed lifetimes and account for losses without creating material. Add a quality constraint and a processing-capacity limit. Explain why a high end-of-use recovery percentage does not alone establish that primary feed demand has vanished.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
